  "designNotes": "Iron ore is over-supplied (1 per 6 ticks against a need of 1 per 12) and the assembler sits in a walled pocket whose only reachable input is the west port, so the compact answer is one mixed wire+plate belt. Either input port takes either ingredient, but a mixed belt only works if each item arrives no faster than 3 wire : 1 plate is consumed; a plain merger lets the surplus plates fill the belt head and the assembler deadlocks (delivers a few circuits, then nothing). Throttle the plates with a splitter that dumps every other plate into the disposal (cheap, small footprint, fragile if rates change), or take the safe build: two separate belts, with the second (wire) line snaking around the top corridor to the assembler's north notch: bigger footprint, higher cost, later first delivery, but no deadlock risk.",
  "par": {
    "footprint": 70,
    "cost": 113,
    "latency": 45
  }
